        "### Reading history\n",
        "\n",
        "Three questions come up constantly, and each has one command.\n",
        "\n",
        "**\"What changed recently?\"**"
      ],
      "id": "OUiVcLgD_NqI"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pZDhEip3_NqI"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "git('git log --oneline --stat')"
      ],
      "id": "pZDhEip3_NqI"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qJYB1MlD_NqJ"
      },
      "source": [
        "**\"What exactly did that commit do?\"** `git show` takes any commit hash. `HEAD` means the current commit, and `HEAD~1` means the one before it."
      ],
      "id": "qJYB1MlD_NqJ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "dgMJ7V-m_NqJ"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "git('git show HEAD~1')"
      ],
      "id": "dgMJ7V-m_NqJ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pOggDG2q_NqJ"
      },
      "source": [
        "**\"What have I changed since my last commit?\"** This is the one to run before every commit. Note the difference between unstaged and staged changes."
      ],
      "id": "pOggDG2q_NqJ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Shi2ziB3_NqJ"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "write('prices.csv', 'item,price\\nponcho,6.00\\nburger,7.50\\nnachos,5.25\\n')\n",
        "\n",
        "git('git diff')              # not staged yet\n",
        "git('git add prices.csv')\n",
        "git('git diff')              # empty now -- it moved to staging\n",
        "git('git diff --staged')     # here it is"
      ],
      "id": "Shi2ziB3_NqJ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "wJxjVIsd_NqJ"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Undoing things\n",
        "\n",
        "Four situations, four different commands. Using the wrong one is how people lose work.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Situation | Command | Destructive? |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Staged something by mistake | `git restore --staged <file>` | No |\n",
        "| Want to throw away an uncommitted edit | `git restore <file>` | **Yes** — the edit is gone |\n",
        "| A commit was wrong, and it is already pushed | `git revert <hash>` | No — adds an undo commit |\n",
        "| \"Just make it look like before\" | `git reset --hard` | **Yes** — avoid it |\n",
        "\n",
        "Unstaging first, since it is the common one:"
      ],
      "id": "wJxjVIsd_NqJ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "zat4fMhz_NqJ"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "git('git restore --staged prices.csv')\n",
        "git('git status --short')     # back to unstaged: the edit still exists\n",
        "git('git add prices.csv')\n",
        "git(\"git commit -m 'Add nachos to price list'\")"
      ],
      "id": "zat4fMhz_NqJ"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "u7CMRgbt_NqK"
      },
      "source": [
        "`git revert` is the one to know for shared work. It does not erase history — it writes a new commit that undoes an old one, so anyone who already pulled stays in sync."
      ],
      "id": "u7CMRgbt_NqK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "WoEySgp4_NqK"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "git('git log --oneline')\n",
        "git('git revert --no-edit HEAD')\n",
        "git('git log --oneline')\n",
        "print(open(os.path.join(SANDBOX, 'prices.csv')).read())"
      ],
      "id": "WoEySgp4_NqK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "k7iHy8aT_NqK"
      },
      "source": [
        "Four commits now: nachos was added, then reverted, and both facts are in the history. That is the behavior you want on a team — nothing silently disappears."
      ],
      "id": "k7iHy8aT_NqK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "vaO7M9jN_NqK"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Two people, one file\n",
        "\n",
        "This is where project weeks go wrong, so let's cause the problem on purpose. A branch is just a movable label pointing at a commit; working on one lets you change files without disturbing `main`."
      ],
      "id": "vaO7M9jN_NqK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "MmQZKyhX_NqK"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "git('git checkout -b cheaper-ponchos')\n",
        "write('prices.csv', 'item,price\\nponcho,4.00\\nburger,7.50\\n')\n",
        "git(\"git commit -am 'Drop poncho price to 4.00'\")\n",
        "\n",
        "git('git checkout main')\n",
        "write('prices.csv', 'item,price\\nponcho,8.00\\nburger,7.50\\n')\n",
        "git(\"git commit -am 'Raise poncho price to 8.00'\")\n",
        "\n",
        "git('git log --oneline --graph --all')"
      ],
      "id": "MmQZKyhX_NqK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "8Hye1wUd_NqK"
      },
      "source": [
        "The same line changed on both branches. Git cannot pick a winner, so merging stops and asks you."
      ],
      "id": "8Hye1wUd_NqK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7-PK46CH_NqK"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "git('git merge cheaper-ponchos')\n",
        "print('--- prices.csv as Git left it ---')\n",
        "print(open(os.path.join(SANDBOX, 'prices.csv')).read())"
      ],
      "id": "7-PK46CH_NqK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "lQFsjSQ0_NqL"
      },
      "source": [
        "Those `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, `>>>>>>>` markers are Git showing you both versions. Resolving means editing the file until it is what you want, removing the markers, then adding and committing. There is no magic command — it is a human decision."
      ],
      "id": "lQFsjSQ0_NqL"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ODZjVpnT_NqL"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "write('prices.csv', 'item,price\\nponcho,6.00\\nburger,7.50\\n')  # the decision\n",
        "git('git add prices.csv')\n",
        "git(\"git commit -m 'Resolve price conflict: settle on 6.00'\")\n",
        "git('git log --oneline --graph')"
      ],
      "id": "ODZjVpnT_NqL"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "iFSKysfd_NqL"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Notebooks make this worse\n",
        "\n",
        "A `.ipynb` is JSON with your code, your outputs, and an execution counter per cell. When two people edit one notebook, the conflict lands inside that JSON and the markers break the file so it will not even open.\n",
        "\n",
        "Rules that keep you out of that hole on the midterm and capstone:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **One owner at a time** for any given notebook. Say so out loud in Discord.\n",
        "2. **Pull before you start working**, not after you finish.\n",
        "3. **Restart and Run All, then commit.** Committed outputs should match committed code.\n",
        "4. Split project work into **separate files** — cleaning, API, analysis — instead of three people in one notebook.\n",
        "5. If a notebook does conflict, the fastest honest fix is usually to keep one side whole, re-apply the other person's cells by hand, and re-run."
      ],
      "id": "iFSKysfd_NqL"
    },
    {
